---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import {url} from '../../data/plugins';
const diagram=(name:string)=>url(`images/physical-modeling/${name}.svg`);
const engines=[
 {id:'tine',name:'Tine',family:'Metal, struck',tone:'Round body. A bell at the edge.',why:'A hammer sets a thin metal tine in motion. A heavier tone bar exchanges energy with it. A magnetic pickup turns that movement into an electrical signal.',labels:['Hammer supplies the initial push.','Tone bar exchanges energy with the tine.','Tine keeps vibrating after the strike.','Magnetic pickup reads the moving metal.'],ours:'Separate tine and tone-bar motion, two transverse directions and a nonlinear magnetic readout. Contact losses and upper-mode decay change with touch.',limit:'A reduced model with tuned coefficients, not a reconstruction of a specific vintage unit.',try:'Start dry. Play the same note softly, then firmly: the bell and attack should change along with the level.'},
 {id:'wurli',name:'Wurli',family:'Reed, struck',tone:'A soft reed that can bite.',why:'The vibrating part is a metal reed. It moves next to a charged electrode: the changing gap changes capacitance. That is a different way of sensing sound from a magnetic pickup.',labels:['Flexible reed, fixed at one end.','Hammer excites the reed.','Electrode senses the changing gap.'],ours:'An independently owned reed model and a geometry-based capacitive readout. It does not run through the Tine magnetic-pickup model.',limit:'The electrical field is approximated; individual reed and amplifier variations are not exhaustively calibrated.',try:'Compare light chords with a stronger single note. Listen for a change in bite, rather than just more volume.'},
 {id:'upright',name:'Upright',family:'Strings, struck',tone:'A piano starts at the hammer.',why:'A felt hammer briefly contacts the strings. The strings transfer energy through a bridge into a soundboard, which moves air. Slightly different strings on one note help give the sound its living texture.',labels:['Felt hammer makes a brief contact.','Strings vibrate with slightly different tuning.','Bridge transfers their motion.','Soundboard helps radiate the sound.'],ours:'Reciprocal hammer–string interaction, up to 96 string modes, unison behavior and a separate 64-mode body/radiation stage. The hammer and string affect each other.',limit:'Reference-note calibration was concentrated around F3–E4. Other registers use modeled and empirical extensions; this is not a measured full piano.',try:'Leave space between chords. Listen to the hard edge of the attack and the softer body that follows it.'},
 {id:'felt',name:'Felt Piano',family:'Strings, softer contact',tone:'The softness begins before the EQ.',why:'A softer contact spreads the impact over a little more time and distance. It excites the upper string vibrations less strongly, especially at gentle velocities.',labels:['Wider, compliant felt contact.','Hammer pushes through the soft layer.','Independent bridge and body response.'],ours:'A separately owned copy of the accepted Upright foundation, with a softer, wider and more dissipative contact layer. Its damping and contact state are not shared with Upright.',limit:'The extra felt parameters are physically motivated voicing choices, not measurements of a particular prepared piano.',try:'Use low-to-medium velocities for a muffled chord bed. Add room only after the dry attack feels right.'},
 {id:'thumb',name:'Thumb',family:'Metal tongue, plucked',tone:'A little metal, a little wood.',why:'A thumb releases a metal tongue held by supports. Several bending motions ring together. The body adds another, gentler resonance underneath the bright initial sound.',labels:['Support controls how the tongue bends.','Metal tongues are plucked at their free ends.','Body adds a softer resonance.'],ours:'A six-mode supported-beam model with a short metallic path, a weaker lingering component and three fixed body resonances. Its attack radiation is gently softened.',limit:'Support geometry, modal balance and decay are reduced approximations. It is not a measured reproduction of one kalimba.',try:'Try small two-note shapes and leave gaps. The short metallic front and quieter body should remain distinct.'},
 {id:'clav',name:'Clav',family:'String, key-driven',tone:'A dark, percussive conversation.',why:'A rubber-tipped key pushes a string against a support. Pickups sense the resulting vibration, while damping keeps the sound tight. Pickup position strongly changes which overtones stand out.',labels:['Rubber-tipped key excites the string.','Support defines the speaking part.','Two pickup positions color the vibration.','Damped section keeps the response short.'],ours:'A finite excitation pulse, 32 string modes, two pickup readouts and short yarn-like loss. Softer excitation and a more centered pickup blend tame the bright edge.',limit:'This uses a reduced pulse/contact approximation, not a complete key–string collision or a particular vintage pickup circuit.',try:'Play short syncopated notes. Start without drive: the darker body should carry the rhythm without a sharp synthetic spike.'},
 {id:'muted',name:'Muted Guitar',family:'String, palm-damped',tone:'A pluck with a hand on the tail.',why:'A pick releases the string while the palm touches it close to the bridge. That contact removes energy from its motion. A short note should still have a pitched body before it disappears.',labels:['Finite pick width shapes the initial pluck.','Magnetic pickup senses string motion.','Palm applies two nearby damping contacts.','Bridge anchors the string.'],ours:'A 32-mode stiff string, finite-width excitation and a magnetic readout. Two passive velocity-damping contacts near the bridge replace the old blanket loss adjustment.',limit:'Current audition candidate. The palm is a reduced two-contact model, not a nonlinear hand simulation or a full six-string guitar.',try:'Compare the short dry groove below with the other engines. Listen for a rounded pluck followed by a quickly damped, still-pitched body.'}
];
---
<Base title="Inside Felt Club — Physical modeling, illustrated | Kim’s Work" description="A beginner-friendly visual guide to seven Felt Club instrument engines: hammers, strings, reeds, pickups and the small differences that make a sound.">
<article class="model-guide wrap">
 <header class="model-intro">
  <p class="eyebrow">THE WORKSHOP / AN ILLUSTRATED DEVELOPMENT JOURNAL</p>
  <h1>Small vibrations.<br/><em>Different worlds.</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">How Felt Club turns a strike, a pluck and a little lost energy into seven instruments. No physics degree required.</p>
  <div class="meta"><span>12-minute read</span><span>14 original diagrams</span><span>7 audio examples</span><span>Updated 21 Nov 2026</span></div>
  <figure class="hero-diagram"><img src={diagram('flow')} width="640" height="320" alt="Sound flows from touch, through a vibrating material and a pickup or body, into effects."/><figcaption>Start here: every engine follows this broad journey. The materials and the way we read their motion make the difference.</figcaption></figure>
  <aside class="status"><strong>A look inside work in progress.</strong> This guide documents the engine work completed through 0.2.37. Felt Club 0.3.0 now makes those engines available with a revoiced factory bank. Tine, Wurli, Upright, Felt Piano, Thumb and Clav have owner-approved listening baselines. Muted Guitar’s latest audition informed the muted-guitar factory set; exact recording equivalence is not claimed. Hardware audio, physical-control and worst-case CPU certification remain pending. See the product manual for current remote-device checks.</aside>
 </header>
 <nav class="chapters" aria-label="Guide chapters"><a href="#basics">The idea</a><a href="#engines">The seven engines</a><a href="#listen">Listen</a><a href="#controls">What to turn</a><a href="#process">How we improve it</a><a href="#sources">Sources & limits</a></nav>
 <section id="basics">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / THE IDEA</p><h2>Build the vibration.<br/>Then decide how to hear it.</h2>
  <p>Instead of playing a recorded note, these engines calculate a simplified vibrating object as you play. A note begins with energy from a hammer, key or pick. That energy moves between parts and gradually escapes as heat and sound.</p>
  <div class="explain-grid">
   <figure><img src={diagram('modes')} width="640" height="320" loading="lazy" alt="Three simple vibrations at different frequencies represent components of a complex sound."/><figcaption><strong>A mode is one way something can vibrate.</strong> Add several together and a tone gains its body, bell or metallic edge. The drawing is schematic, not measured audio.</figcaption></figure>
   <figure><img src={diagram('readout')} width="640" height="320" loading="lazy" alt="A pickup senses moving metal; a piano soundboard transfers vibration to air."/><figcaption><strong>Motion is only half the story.</strong> A pickup converts movement into electricity. An acoustic piano uses a bridge and soundboard. The same vibration can sound different through a different readout.</figcaption></figure>
   <figure><img src={diagram('touch')} width="640" height="320" loading="lazy" alt="A soft and a firm strike have different illustrative attack shapes."/><figcaption><strong>Velocity means how hard you play.</strong> It can change contact and upper vibrations as well as loudness. Turning a quiet recording up is not the same as playing harder.</figcaption></figure>
   <figure><img src={diagram('decay')} width="640" height="320" loading="lazy" alt="A rapidly decaying vibration and a longer envelope illustrate different damping."/><figcaption><strong>Damping means energy leaving the system.</strong> Different frequencies can die away at different speeds. Shortening the whole note with a volume envelope cannot reproduce every material change.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
 </section>
 <section id="engines"><p class="eyebrow">02 / A SMALL COLLECTION OF DIFFERENT MACHINES</p><h2>Seven engines.<br/>Seven ways to begin a note.</h2><p>These are conceptual cutaways, not scale engineering drawings. Follow the numbered parts beneath each picture, then open the implementation notes if you want the technical detail.</p>
 {engines.map((e,i)=><section class="engine" id={e.id}>
   <div class="engine-heading"><span class="number">{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><div><p class="eyebrow">{e.family}</p><h3>{e.name}</h3></div><span class:list={['badge',{'candidate':e.id==='muted'}]}>{e.id==='muted'?'In audition':'Listening approved'}</span></div>
   <div class="engine-grid"><figure><img src={diagram(e.id)} width="640" height="320" loading="lazy" alt={`${e.name} concept: ${e.why}`}/><figcaption><ol>{e.labels.map(l=><li>{l}</li>)}</ol></figcaption></figure><div class="engine-copy"><h4>{e.tone}</h4><p>{e.why}</p><p class="try"><strong>Listen for this</strong>{e.try}</p><details><summary>What we implemented</summary><p>{e.ours}</p><p class="limitation">{e.limit}</p></details></div></div>
 </section>)}
 </section>
 <section id="listen"><p class="eyebrow">03 / HEAR THE MATERIAL</p><h2>Same small phrase.<br/>A different response.</h2><p>These original examples run through the actual native Felt Club 0.2.37 plugin. All seven use the same 92 BPM phrase, note timing and velocities 45–75, with effects off. They are illustrative dry recipes, not factory preset auditions or recordings of acoustic instruments.</p><p class="small">Each complete take uses one fixed gain to reach −24 dBFS RMS. This makes material differences easier to compare but removes absolute level differences between engines. Notes are not individually normalized. AAC previews are encoded from 48 kHz stereo native renders.</p>
 <div class="audio-grid">{engines.map(e=><div class="audio-card"><div><h3>{e.name}</h3><span>{e.id==='muted'?'Audition candidate':'Approved engine · shared test phrase'}</span></div><audio controls preload="none" aria-label={`${e.name}: dry 92 BPM phrase`}><source src={url(`audio/felt-club-modeling/${e.id}.m4a`)} type="audio/mp4"/>Your browser does not support audio playback.</audio><a href={url(`audio/felt-club-modeling/${e.id}.m4a`)}>Open audio ↗</a></div>)}</div>
 </section>
 <section id="controls"><p class="eyebrow">04 / FROM THE MODEL TO YOUR FINGERS</p><h2>Start dry. Play softly.<br/>Add the room last.</h2>
 <div class="control-grid"><div><h3>Shape the instrument first.</h3><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Touch.</strong> Try low, medium and firm velocities before reaching for effects.</li><li><strong>Contact.</strong> Hammer and model-character controls influence how the note begins. Their meaning depends on the engine.</li><li><strong>Body and Tone.</strong> Find the balance between the initial edge and the resonating body.</li><li><strong>Space and movement.</strong> Add a little room, delay or autopan after the dry sound works. MAIN Noise is a separate continuous hiss; Mechanical is strike-related, and the deeper Bark/Board/Mute control remains on MODEL.</li></ol></div><figure><img src={diagram('envelope')} width="640" height="320" loading="lazy" alt="ADSR level shape: attack, decay, sustain and release, with the key release marked."/><figcaption><strong>Two different kinds of decay.</strong> ADSR shapes the played level. The modeled material is also losing energy inside that shape. A long Release lets a tail remain; it does not add energy back into a dead string.</figcaption></figure></div>
 <aside class="status"><strong>Why not make Roll Dice change everything?</strong> Felt Club keeps the selected engine and protects locked controls. Engine-specific ranges keep the starting character recognizable. Body and Release are drawn together, and generated color effects are bounded. That reduces extreme combinations; it does not promise that every roll suits every song.</aside>
 </section>
 <section id="process"><p class="eyebrow">05 / THE WORK BEHIND THE SOUND</p><h2>One engine at a time.<br/>Then check the others stayed put.</h2><div class="control-grid"><figure><img src={diagram('isolation')} width="640" height="320" loading="lazy" alt="Seven independent engine blocks connect to common musical controls."/><figcaption>Each engine owns its vibrating state. Shared musical controls do not mean shared hammer, reed, string or pickup state.</figcaption></figure><div><h3>Our improvement loop</h3><ol class="steps"><li>Identify the mechanism: what is struck or plucked, and how is it heard?</li><li>Compare dry attacks, held notes, releases and several registers at varied velocities.</li><li>Change the target engine, then render an identical MIDI phrase before and after.</li><li>Check stability, note release, state recall and unchanged output from the other engines.</li><li>Get listening feedback. Repeat before adding production effects.</li></ol></div></div>
 <details><summary>Current checks and what they do not establish</summary><p>The 0.3.0 release builds for native, ARM32 and ARM64. SDK validation and automated regression checks pass; the native SDK host reports 19 passes, no warnings and no failures. At matched sound settings, all seven engines produce sample-identical native test output to the final 0.2.37 baseline at 44.1, 48 and 96 kHz.</p><p>Muted Guitar’s contact update is checked against sequential passive projections: contact removes modal velocity energy rather than injecting it. A separate check confirms stronger palm pressure reduces the normalized held tail. These are specific numerical properties, not proof of a complete real guitar.</p><p>Desktop tests do not establish actual MPC audio quality, physical Q-Link behavior, worst-case polyphony or CPU cost. Those remain separate hardware checks. Owner approval means a listening baseline was accepted; it is not a laboratory match to a commercial instrument.</p></details>
 </section>
 <section id="sources"><p class="eyebrow">06 / FOLLOW THE THREAD</p><h2>Research informs the model.<br/>Listening keeps it honest.</h2><p>Felt Club uses reduced physical models alongside empirical voicing. A few useful physical mechanisms matter more here than a claim to simulate every moving part. The figures on this page are original explanatory drawings. No third-party recording, commercial plugin render or reference sample is included in the audio examples.</p>
 <div class="reference-grid"><div><h3>Electric pianos</h3><p>Mechanical excitation and distinct magnetic/capacitive sensing informed the Tine and Wurli approach.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.dafx17.eca.ed.ac.uk/papers/DAFx17_paper_79.pdf">Pfeifle · Real-time Physical Model of a Wurlitzer and Rhodes Electric Piano (2017) ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://afalaize.github.io/posts/rhodes/">Falaize · Electromechanical piano model and comparisons ↗</a></li></ul></div><div><h3>Piano strings and radiation</h3><p>String coupling, unisons and body response guide the Upright foundation; Felt adds its own softer contact.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.speech.kth.se/music/5_lectures/weinreic/strings.html">Weinreich · Coupled piano strings, KTH lecture ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://zenodo.org/records/2573232">BiVib · Open vibroacoustic instrument data ↗</a></li></ul></div><div><h3>Plucked and key-driven strings</h3><p>Bridge-side palm contact and pickup position motivate the string models. The solver on this page is our reduced implementation.</p><ul><li><a href="https://speech.di.uoa.gr/ICMC-SMC-2014/images/VOL_2/1483.pdf">Biral, d’Alessandro & Freed · Palm-mute pressure profiles (2014) ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1687-6180-2013-103">Clavinet modeling research (2013) ↗</a></li></ul></div><div><h3>Metal tongues</h3><p>Supported-beam vibration is a starting point for Thumb, with separately voiced metal and body paths.</p><ul><li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22280717/">Kalimba mechanics research · Abstract and citation ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://dael.euracoustics.org/confs/acoustics2008/data/articles/000215.pdf">Acoustics 2008 · Lamellophone vibration research ↗</a></li></ul></div></div>
 <details><summary>Musical references and their limits</summary><p>Public Keyscape demonstrations helped frame early EP and piano listening. Nas’s “One Love” guided the softer Thumb direction; Stevie Wonder’s “Superstition” guided the darker Clav direction. Hikaru Utada’s “Fly Me to the Moon” is the Muted reference, provisionally the 2000 original. These are musical direction references, not isolated measurements or evidence of the equipment used on a record. No exact commercial-product or recording match is claimed.</p></details>
 <p class="closing">From the workshop, with room to improve.<br/><a href={url('plugins/felt-club/')}>Explore Felt Club 0.3.0 →</a></p>
 </section>
</article>
